  <div class="faq-section">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How do I know if my QA strategy is working?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">The clearest signal is your escaped defect rate – how many bugs reach production despite having a QA process. Secondary signals: how long it takes to find out a release broke something, whether CI failures are treated as urgent or ignored, and whether your QA engineer spends more time writing test cases or chasing down flaky scripts.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">What should be in a test strategy document?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">A test strategy document should cover scope (what you test and what you do not), risk model (what to prioritize), tooling decisions, environment strategy, metrics, and ownership. It should be short enough that people actually read it – not a 40-page enterprise document.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How do you integrate QA into agile sprints?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">QA belongs in every phase of the sprint: in planning (writing test cases alongside stories), during development (testing features as they become available), in the sprint review (demonstrating tested functionality), and in retrospective (identifying quality process improvements).</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">What is the testing pyramid?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">The testing pyramid is a model for distributing test coverage across three layers: a large base of unit tests, a middle layer of integration and API tests, and a small top layer of end-to-end UI tests. Most teams have this inverted – too many slow UI tests, not enough fast unit and API tests.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-question">How often should we review our QA process?</div>
      <div class="faq-answer">A lightweight process review belongs in every retrospective. A deeper review – covering tooling, coverage trends, escaped defect analysis – is worth doing every quarter.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

